INT. REGIONAL AIRPORT BAGGAGE CLAIM - NIGHT

A dead carousel. One flickering fluorescent tube. A vending machine humming a note a half-step flat. Rain thrashes the empty glass hall. The last flight in was four hours ago.

THADDEUS BRANSON-OYELARAN (50s, an immaculate overcoat, the posture of a man who has complained to managers before) stands with arms crossed beside a single battered suitcase. It is not his. He has decided it is his.

GREGORIO "GIGI" PETRAKIS-NWOSU (50s, a blazer the color of canned peaches, a rolling cart of lost luggage) pushes through a swing door, whistling, and stops. He sees Thaddeus. Thaddeus sees him.

GIGI
Sir, the airport is closed.

THADDEUS
I'm aware.

GIGI
Then why are you in it?

THADDEUS
Because my bag is in it.

GIGI
This is a lost-and-found area. Your bag is lost.

THADDEUS
My bag is right here.

GIGI
That's not your bag.

THADDEUS
It's the bag I was given.

GIGI
By whom?

THADDEUS
By the carousel.

Gigi studies the dead carousel. It doesn't move. He studies the suitcase. It's the color of mud, with a duct-taped corner.

GIGI
Sir, that bag has been here since Tuesday.

THADDEUS
Then it's been waiting for me.

GIGI
It's tagged to a Mr. Hassan Abdi-Lindgren, Gate Nine.

THADDEUS
I'm a Hassan in spirit.

GIGI
Are you?

THADDEUS
(a long pause)
No.

He crosses his arms. The other man does not uncross his. They stand in a faint electrical hum, two statues who each know the other won't blink.

GIGI
(to herself)
Lord, give me strength.

THADDEUS
(to himself)
Lord, give me my bag.

Gigi pulls a clipboard from the cart. Writes. Rips off a form. Holds it out.

GIGI
Fill out Form 31-B.

THADDEUS
Of what?

GIGI
Of your grievance.

He takes the form. Reads it. Looks up.

THADDEUS
It's in triplicate.

GIGI
We're a serious airport.

THADDEUS
You have one gate.

GIGI
It's a serious gate.

He snaps his fingers. A pen sails out of his pocket and lands in the other man's hand. Thaddeus stares at it. It's a ballpoint chained to a small rubber frog.

THADDEUS
What is this?

GIGI
Our pen.

THADDEUS
It's a frog.

GIGI
It's a frog pen. It's how we know you're honest.

Thaddeus clicks it. Starts writing. The frog dangles. Gigi leans over, reading upside down.

GIGI (CONT'D)
That's not a grievance. That's a poem.

THADDEUS
It's a long grievance.

GIGI
(reading)
"Oh, suitcase, thou art mud."

THADDEUS
Stop reading.

GIGI
"Oh, suitcase, thou art lost, yet found, yet mine."

THADDEUS
It's not for you!

GIGI
It's on my form!

Thaddeus tears the form in half. Gigi gasps. He snatches the halves and tries to fit them together.

THADDEUS
You can't do that!

^GIGI
You can't do that!

They stop. A beat. The vending machine hums.

THADDEUS
(to the empty hall)
Is there anyone else?

GIGI
(to the empty hall)
No.

THADDEUS
(to Gigi)
Why are you here?

GIGI
Because someone has to be.

THADDEUS
For a suitcase?

GIGI
For anyone's suitcase.

A long silence. Thaddeus looks at the bag. He looks at the torn form. The fluorescent tube buzzes, dims, and recovers.

THADDEUS
It isn't mine.

GIGI
I know.

THADDEUS
Mine had a yellow ribbon.

GIGI
I know. It's in the back. It came in Tuesday. I was just waiting to see who would show up for the wrong one first.

THADDEUS
That's cruel.

GIGI
That's customer service.

He rolls his cart to the swing door. Holds it open with his hip. The other man stands for a moment. Then he picks up the muddy suitcase, hugs it once, sets it down gently, and follows him out.
